    <body>
      <sec id="article-26892.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Penicillin allergy is commonly reported, yet most patients with a history of penicillin allergy can ultimately tolerate penicillin after drug allergy evaluation. Misclassification of penicillin allergy has clinical implications, including suboptimal antibiotic selection, increased use of broad-spectrum antibiotics, and a higher risk of antimicrobial resistance and adverse outcomes. This course discusses the epidemiology, immunologic mechanisms, and common clinical manifestations of penicillin allergy. This activity reviews the role of &#x003b2;-lactam side-chain structures in drug cross-reactivity and introduces several validated risk-stratification tools to guide the evaluation and management of prior penicillin allergy in adult and pediatric patients. This course provides healthcare professionals with knowledge and practical tools to obtain an allergy history, identify candidates for penicillin skin testing, and consider beta-lactam antibiotics other than penicillin. Roles of the interprofessional healthcare team in managing penicillin allergy are presented, along with opportunities to optimize interprofessional care through accurate allergy assessment, communication, and collaboration.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify clinical features that can help distinguish IgE-mediated from non&#x02013;IgE-mediated penicillin reactions based on historical questions.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Compare cross-reactivity risks among various types of &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics, based on chemical structure features and published data.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate a patient&#x02019;s penicillin allergy history using validated risk stratification tools to guide allergy management decisions and antibiotic selection.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with the interprofessional team to improve patient outcomes by appropriately identifying, educating, and treating patients with penicillin allergy.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=26892&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=26892">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26892.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Penicillin allergy encompasses a spectrum of immunologic reactions, ranging from immediate IgE-mediated responses such as urticaria, angioedema, and anaphylaxis to delayed T-cell&#x02013;mediated reactions, including maculopapular exanthems and severe cutaneous adverse reactions. Penicillin allergy is the most commonly reported medication allergy, affecting approximately 8% to 10% of patients in the United States. However, the prevalence of true IgE-mediated hypersensitivity is much lower. Careful clinical history, supplemented by appropriate testing, such as skin testing when indicated and graded drug challenges, is essential for an accurate diagnosis. Even among patients with confirmed IgE-mediated allergy to penicillin (such as urticaria), most will develop tolerance to penicillin after a decade.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r2">[2]</xref>[AAAAI. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/penicillin-allergy">Penicillin Allergy FAQ</ext-link>]</p>
        <p>An inaccurate allergy label has significant clinical implications. &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics are commonly prescribed in clinical practice. Consequently, understanding true penicillin allergy and its relationship to other &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics can lead to improved antibiotic stewardship, reduced use of&#x000a0;broad-spectrum antibiotics, and a lower likelihood of developing multidrug-resistant&#x000a0;organisms.&#x000a0;Systematic, evidence-based algorithms are available to help health care teams decide whether to use nonpenicillin &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics in patients with IgE-mediated penicillin allergy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Recent guidelines emphasize the importance of <italic toggle="yes">delabeling</italic> inappropriate penicillin allergies through structured evaluation, as this approach improves antibiotic stewardship and patient outcomes. Increasing recognition of the low prevalence of true allergy and the safety of diagnostic testing has led to broader efforts to integrate assessment of penicillin allergy into routine clinical care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26892.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Penicillin is considered a type of &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotic because its chemical structure contains a &#x003b2;-lactam ring. Other &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics include penicillin derivatives (ampicillin, amoxicillin), cephalosporins, carbapenems, and aztreonam (a monobactam). These antibiotics differ structurally in their <italic toggle="yes">R</italic> side chains. Research findings show that these <italic toggle="yes">R</italic> side chains are more likely than the &#x003b2;-lactam&#x000a0;ring to affect a patient's tolerance to another &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotic. Specifically, individuals with IgE-mediated penicillin allergy are less likely to experience an allergic reaction when they receive a &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotic with an <italic toggle="yes">R</italic> side chain dissimilar to the penicillin <italic toggle="yes">R</italic> side chain.&#x000a0;Although &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics were historically avoided in patients with penicillin allergy due to concerns about cross-reactivity, recent research findings indicate that these individuals often tolerate more nonpenicillin &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics than previously thought.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Anaphylaxis, urticaria, angioedema, and wheezing are some manifestations of IgE-mediated (type 1) hypersensitivity reactions to penicillin. These manifestations can also be accompanied by vomiting or diarrhea. IgE-mediated reactions typically occur within 1 hour of the last administered dose of penicillin. Histories inconsistent with IgE-mediated drug allergy include isolated headache or diarrhea, especially if they occur more than 2 hours after the last dose of penicillin. Morbilliform drug eruption is another&#x000a0;common manifestation of penicillin drug allergy, but penicillin-specific IgE antibodies are not responsible for it. Instead, morbilliform drug eruption is typically caused by T-cell&#x02013;mediated (type 4) hypersensitivity. The distinction between IgE-mediated and non&#x02013;IgE-mediated reactions is clinically relevant because penicillin skin testing (which detects histamine release by mast cells triggered by penicillin-specific IgE antibodies) is not useful or indicated for non&#x02013;IgE-mediated reactions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r3">[3]</xref>[World Allergy Organization. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.worldallergy.org/component/content/article/drug-allergies-thong-b-vervloet-d-torres-jaen-mj-updated-2021">Drug Allergy</ext-link>]</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26892.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Approximately 10% of patients report an adverse reaction to a penicillin-related antibiotic, but research findings indicate that more than 90% of these patients can tolerate penicillins. Even among patients with confirmed IgE-mediated allergy to penicillin (such as urticaria), most will develop tolerance to penicillin after a decade.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/penicillin-allergy">Penicillin Allergy FAQ</ext-link><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Analyses of drug-related anaphylaxis deaths in the United States found penicillins were the most commonly identified culprit, and adults were more likely than children to experience severe or fatal penicillin-induced anaphylaxis. Benign,&#x000a0;delayed allergic reactions (occurring more than 1 hour after the last administered dose, such as morbilliform drug eruption) are more common than IgE-mediated allergic reactions to penicillin. Aminopenicillins (eg, amoxicillin, ampicillin) are associated with morbilliform drug eruptions in up to 7% of patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Early estimates of penicillin&#x02013;cephalosporin cross-reactivity allergy were estimated at 8%. However, this estimate was inflated because it focused only on specific cephalosporins, and before 1980, contamination of cephalosporins with penicillin occurred. After 1980, data on penicillin&#x02013;cephalosporin cross-reactivity indicate a range of 2.0% to 4.8%, which is similar to the incidence of adverse reactions to structurally dissimilar medications in patients with prior drug allergies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Findings from several systematic reviews indicate that clinical cross-reactivity between carbapenems and penicillin is low. Results from a 2014 systematic review including 838 patients estimated that this cross-reactivity affected 4.3% of patients (95% CI, 3.1%-5.9%). More recently, results from a 2019 systematic review and meta-analysis including 1127 patients reported a risk of cross-reactivity to any carbapenem of 0.87% (95%&#x000a0;CI, 0.32%-2.32%). Clinically significant cross-reactivity has not been identified between penicillin and aztreonam.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26892.s5" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>For a patient presenting with concern for an acute allergic reaction after penicillin administration, ensuring the patient's clinical stability (eg, airway, breathing, circulation) is a priority. After the patient is stabilized, history taking can more thoroughly address symptoms such as respiratory distress, dysphagia, rash, pruritus, vomiting, diarrhea, or confusion.&#x000a0;The medical history should also include the interval between penicillin administration and symptom onset, the route of administration, and any other medications recently administered. The initial examination should focus on airway, breathing, and circulation, including assessment for stridor, difficulty&#x000a0;swallowing or clearing secretions, and angioedema of the lips, tongue, or oropharynx. Cardiopulmonary examination findings such as tachycardia or wheezing indicate a more severe allergic reaction. Although an acute allergic reaction to penicillin typically involves skin findings (eg, generalized urticaria, flushing, pruritus, or angioedema), cutaneous symptoms may be absent in a small percentage of patients experiencing anaphylaxis.</p>
        <p>For a patient who reports a history of penicillin allergy because of a previous exposure to penicillin, important historical questions should address the route of penicillin administration, severity of the allergic reaction (eg, anaphylaxis or isolated cutaneous symptoms), and the time interval between the start of penicillin administration and the onset of symptoms. Reactions that occur within 1 hour of the last administered dose are suggestive of immediate hypersensitivity. Additional questions can assess how the reaction was managed, such as whether epinephrine was used and whether symptoms improved after its administration. Clinicians should also clarify any underlying illness and other exposures to identify additional factors that may have contributed to the reaction. Inquiring about the patient's&#x000a0;reactions to &#x003b2;-lactams (eg, cephalosporins)&#x000a0;before and after the penicillin-associated reaction can help the health care team assess the patient's risk of an allergic reaction to nonpenicillin &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26892.s6" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>For a patient presenting with a possible acute allergic reaction after penicillin administration, laboratory evaluation should include an acute-phase serum tryptase level as early as possible during a suspected anaphylactic event. Ideally, the tryptase level should be drawn within 2 hours of symptom onset. However, empiric treatment for anaphylaxis should not be delayed or withheld while awaiting tryptase test results.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>For a patient who reports a history of penicillin allergy from a prior exposure, validated tools are available to help health care teams use historical questions to risk-stratify drug allergy (including penicillin allergy).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;These algorithms can determine if a patient has a <italic toggle="yes">low-risk</italic> drug allergy. For example, the Penicillin Allergy Fast Assessment (PEN-FAST) clinical decision rule for patients reporting a history of penicillin allergy contains 4 questions (time since reaction&#x000a0;&#x02264;5 years [F], anaphylaxis or angioedema [A], severe cutaneous adverse reaction [S], and whether pharmacotherapy was required to treat the reaction [T]). Each "yes" answer is allotted 1 to 2 points depending on the specific question.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;A PEN-FAST score of greater than 3 is associated with a negative predictive value of 96.3% (95%&#x000a0;CI, 94.1%&#x02013;97.8%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;Penicillin allergy evaluation in children often relies on structured clinical pathways&#x000a0;and institutional risk-assessment tools developed at centers such as Vanderbilt.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r12">[12]</xref>[Children's Hospital of Philadelphia. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.chop.edu/clinical-pathway/penicillin-drug-allergy-clinical-pathway">Clinical Pathway for the Management of Children With a Reported Penicillin Drug Allergy</ext-link>]&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Procedures to evaluate penicillin allergy include penicillin skin testing and drug challenge, which are ideally performed electively, when patients are well and not in immediate need of antibiotic therapy. Penicillin skin testing detects the presence or absence of penicillin-specific IgE antibodies. For this reason, penicillin skin testing is not indicated for nonallergic adverse reactions to penicillin or clearly non&#x02013;IgE-mediated symptoms such as isolated headache. Penicillin skin testing should only be performed by health care personnel trained to administer the&#x000a0;test, interpret test results, and respond to anaphylaxis,&#x000a0;a rare&#x000a0;adverse effect&#x000a0;of the test.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;In certain circumstances (such as a history of non&#x02013;IgE-mediated reactions), a single or multidose drug challenge may be appropriate. The Allergy Immunology Joint Task Force on Practice Parameters has additional information about these procedures, including when one type of penicillin allergy evaluation may be recommended over another.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r15">[15]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26892.s7" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>For a patient presenting with concern for an acute allergic reaction after penicillin administration, appropriate treatment depends on the severity of the reaction and whether an IgE- or non&#x02013;IgE-mediated reaction is suspected. For a patient with isolated cutaneous symptoms of an&#x000a0;IgE-mediated reaction (eg, pruritus or hives), treatment with a histamine type 1 receptor (H1) antihistamine, such as cetirizine, is adequate. However, the patient should be closely monitored for the development of new symptoms indicative of angioedema, hypotension, or other organ-system involvement. In comparison, a patient experiencing anaphylaxis requires immediate, first-line treatment with intramuscular epinephrine (0.01 mg/kg from a 1:1000 [1 mg/mL] solution, up to a maximum of 0.5 mg in adults and 0.3 mg in prepubertal children) in the mid-anterolateral aspect of the thigh. Intramuscular epinephrine can be repeated every 5 minutes as needed, or sooner if clinically indicated. If repeated dosing of intramuscular epinephrine is necessary,&#x000a0;an additional dose is usually required, and rarely 2. Supportive intravenous fluids are indicated in a patient who remains hypotensive after intramuscular epinephrine administration.</p>
        <p>Patients with symptoms refractory to intramuscular epinephrine and intravenous fluids should be treated with an epinephrine infusion, titrated to clinical response and data from continuous hemodynamic monitoring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Beyond epinephrine, other therapies, including H1 antihistamines (eg, cetirizine or diphenhydramine), bronchodilators, and corticosteroids, are adjunctive. For example, H1 antihistamines can be useful for treating pruritus or urticaria that persists despite an overall clinical response to intramuscular epinephrine. Bronchodilators can help relieve bronchoconstriction but are not a substitute for intramuscular epinephrine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>For patients with a history of penicillin allergy following prior exposure, validated tools can help healthcare teams risk-stratify the likelihood of true drug allergy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r3">[3]</xref> Based on the results, management options may include a graded drug challenge or induction of tolerance (desensitization) procedure. These procedures should only be performed by trained and skilled health care personnel prepared to respond to anaphylaxis, a possible adverse effect of the procedure. Induction of tolerance procedures is contraindicated in patients with severe delayed reactions, including hemolytic anemia, acute interstitial nephritis, and severe cutaneous reactions (eg, Stevens-Johnson syndrome or drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms [DRESS]).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26892.s8" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of penicillin allergy includes:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Carcinoid syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chronic urticaria</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hereditary or nonallergic angioedema</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lichen planus&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mastocytosis and mast cell activation syndromes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neuroendocrine tumors</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Scrombroidosis (fish allergy)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Viral exanthem</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26892.s9" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Among patients experiencing&#x000a0;anaphylaxis after administration of penicillin, an estimated 90% of these patients respond to a single dose of epinephrine (especially if epinephrine is administered quickly after the onset of symptoms).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Delayed administration of epinephrine is associated with increased risk of biphasic anaphylaxis and death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;For patients with a reported history of penicillin allergy due to a previous exposure, prognosis depends on the type of allergic reaction experienced. Among patients with confirmed IgE-mediated penicillin allergy, most will develop penicillin tolerance after a decade.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref>[AAAAI. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/penicillin-allergy">Penicillin Allergy FAQ</ext-link>] Many validated risk stratification tools for penicillin allergy consider the length of time since the reported allergic reaction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;In contrast to IgE-mediated penicillin allergy, a history of a severe delayed reaction to penicillin (such as hemolytic anemia, acute interstitial nephritis, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, or drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms [DRESS]) is generally not expected to resolve over time.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The chemical side chain structures of &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics (including penicillin, cephalosporins, carbapenems, and aztreonam) affect the tolerance of other &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics. Consequently, patients with a history of penicillin allergy can receive nonpenicillin &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics, with or without evaluation procedures such as skin testing or drug challenge. Evidence-based clinical decision tools are available to help health care teams determine whether to administer nonpenicillin &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics to patients with IgE-mediated penicillin allergy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26892.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Some research findings indicate that patients with a history of penicillin allergy are more likely to have longer hospital stays and to develop vancomycin-resistant <italic toggle="yes">Enterococcus&#x000a0;</italic>or methicillin-resistant <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus</italic>
<italic toggle="yes">aureus </italic>infections. Results from other studies indicate that patients with a history of penicillin allergy have an increased risk of developing a surgical site infection related to suboptimal antibiotic choice.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r22">[22]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26892.s11" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>A patient with a history of penicillin allergy should be advised to avoid penicillin until the allergy can be evaluated by a health care clinician trained in assessing drug allergy. For example, an allergist-immunologist can help a patient and other members of the healthcare team understand the antibiotic options available to the patient after considering factors such as the type and timing of the penicillin-associated reaction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r2">[2]</xref>[AAAAI.&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/penicillin-allergy">Penicillin Allergy FAQ</ext-link>]&#x000a0;For a patient with a family history of penicillin allergy but no personal history of an adverse reaction to penicillin, patient counseling should include education that penicillin allergy does not follow a predictable pattern of inheritance. A patient does not need to avoid penicillin preemptively if a family member is allergic to it.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref>[AAAAI.&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/penicillin-allergy">Penicillin Allergy FAQ</ext-link>]&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26892.s12" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>A 2022 update from the Allergy and Immunology Joint Task Force on drug allergy suggested that patients with a history of penicillin or cephalosporin allergy can receive a carbapenem without testing or additional precautions. However, this suggestion does not apply to patients with a penicillin or cephalosporin allergy that involves a severe delayed cutaneous or organ-involved reaction (eg, Stevens-Johnson syndrome or acute interstitial nephritis).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients with a history of penicillin or cephalosporin allergy can receive aztreonam without prior testing, except for those with a history of ceftazidime allergy. Because ceftazidime and aztreonam share a structurally identical chemical side chain, more caution with aztreonam is advised for patients with a ceftazidime-specific allergy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26892.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Approximately 10% of patients report an adverse reaction to a penicillin-related antibiotic. While true IgE-mediated penicillin allergy can cause life-threatening anaphylaxis, research indicates that over 90% of patients reporting a penicillin allergy can eventually tolerate penicillins after further clinical evaluation. Validated tools are available to help interdisciplinary health care teams risk-stratify a patient's history of penicillin allergy and identify individuals who no longer need to be classified as allergic to penicillin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Because a penicillin allergy history has been associated with increased risk of developing infections with antibiotic-resistant organisms (eg, vancomycin-resistant&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Enterococcus&#x000a0;</italic>and&#x000a0;methicillin-resistant&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus</italic>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">aureus</italic>), surgical site infections, and other adverse health outcomes, evaluating whether a patient's penicillin allergy remains clinically active can potentially enhance antibiotic choices for the patient and thereby improve health outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>All health care team members (including clinicians, nurses, pharmacists, and other health professionals) should ask patients about their drug allergies for patient safety. Likewise, all healthcare team members should also be informed if a clinical evaluation (eg, a medically supervised drug challenge) determines that a patient's penicillin allergy can be removed from the electronic health record. Additionally, patient bracelets with medical information should be updated if there are any changes in drug allergy status.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26892.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Patient-centered care places the patient's well-being and preferences at the forefront of all decisions. Effective interdisciplinary communication and care coordination across the health care team regarding penicillin allergy status enhances patient care and outcomes. Clinician responsibilities include providing medical expertise on appropriate antibiotic therapy and on the clinical evaluation of drug allergy, while incorporating patient needs and addressing any patient concerns or questions about penicillin allergy. The nursing team's role in closely monitoring vital signs, observing for early warning signs of adverse reactions, and immediately reporting any concerns is critical in the treatment of acute allergic reactions to penicillin as well as in procedures to evaluate or treat penicillin allergy (eg, skin testing, drug challenge, or induction of tolerance). Pharmacists can offer valuable insights into medication management and into the structural similarities and differences among &#x003b2;-lactam antibiotics, including cephalosporins, carbapenems, and aztreonam. Strong communication and collaboration within the interdisciplinary team are critical to minimizing patient harm and optimizing patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
